★ [saf-beliefs-248] (confidence: 0.73)
  "Feedback-Loop Contamination Destroys the Independence Needed for Post-Deployment Verification" — A Belief within safetyist discourse that AI systems whose outputs shape the data they will later be evaluated against make post-deployment output auditing structurally circular.
Encompasses: predictive policing systems generating arrest data that confirms their own predictions, credit-scoring models that deny applications and thereby prevent denied populations from generating default data, pretrial risk tools whose detention recommendations produce the case outcomes used to validate accuracy.
  Vulnerability: Sophisticated causal inference methods—including instrumental variable designs, randomized controlled audits, and natural experiments exploiting policy discontinuities—can in principle recover uncontaminated ground truth even within feedback-affected domains, meaning the circularity is a tractable estimation problem rather than a structural impossibility that 'destroys' verification capacity.
★ [saf-beliefs-043] (confidence: 0.72)
  "Most Real-World AI Harms Go Unrecorded Because Tracking Relies on Media Reports" — A Belief within safetyist discourse that relying on media reports for tracking AI incidents leads to significant underreporting of real-world harms. 
Encompasses: The gap between reported data and actual risk prevalence.
  Vulnerability: Media-sourced incident databases like AIAAIC and AIID already apply structured curation beyond raw reporting, and research on analogous domains (e.g., software CVEs, medical device recalls) suggests that mandatory reporting systems often suffer from definitional inconsistency, chronic under-enforcement, and organizational gaming—meaning the underreporting gap may be far narrower, or differently distributed, than assumed.
★ [saf-beliefs-122] (confidence: 0.68)
  "AI Performs Consequential Acts That No One Bears Responsibility For" — A Belief within safetyist discourse that the legal and ethical void created when AI performs tasks traditionally requiring human moral agency, but cannot assume the consequences of those actions. 
Encompasses: Situations where AI automates tasks like signing legal documents or accepting professional liability, highlighting the absence of a clear entity to bear the resulting consequences.
  Vulnerability: The accountability gap is not inherent to AI systems but is a remediable artifact of legal lag: existing doctrines of vicarious liability, product liability, and respondeat superior can already be extended to cover AI-caused harms, and jurisdictions such as the EU are actively doing so through the AI Liability Directive, suggesting the "void" is transitional rather than structural.
